                        <history>
                            <origin>
                                <p>Based on its contents, script, and decoration, Moberg argues that the sequentiary/hymnal may have been produced in England. The hymn <quote>Gaude Dei genitrix</quote> (<locus from="2r" to="2r" scheme="folios"/>) and the following trope <quote>Gloriosa spes reorum</quote> are both considered to be of English origin, while the melodic scheme of the sequence corresponds to that of <quote>Laudes Deo devotas</quote>, a sequence of English provenance rarely attested in Nordic sources. These features point to English liturgical influence and a possible connection with English ecclesiastical circles in Sweden; <bibl>
                                        <title ref="https://www.manuscripta.se/bibl/101526" type="short">Moberg (1927)</title>
                                        <citedRange unit="page" from="22" to="23"/>
                                    </bibl>; <bibl>
                                        <title ref="https://www.manuscripta.se/bibl/101394" type="short">Moberg (1947)</title>
                                        <citedRange unit="page" from="91" to="91"/>
                                    </bibl>.</p>
                                <p>Moberg later proposed that the manuscript may have been intended for use in <placeName ref="https://www.manuscripta.se/place/100106">Strängnäs</placeName>, citing <quote>Christi miles gloriosus</quote> (<locus from="16v" to="16v" scheme="folios"/>), associated with the feast of St Vincent, which was celebrated as a festum duplex in Strängnäs; <bibl>
                                        <title ref="https://www.manuscripta.se/bibl/101394" type="short">Moberg (1947)</title>
                                        <citedRange unit="page" from="92" to="92"/>
                                        <citedRange unit="page" from="278" to="278"/>
                                    </bibl>. In his earlier work, however, he also considered a possible origin among English Franciscans.  </p>
                                <p>The attribution to Skokloster is probably erroneous, apparently deriving from <persName ref="https://www.manuscripta.se/person/102552">
                                        Martin 
                                        Aschaneus </persName>; <bibl>
                                        <title ref="https://www.manuscripta.se/bibl/101526" type="short">Moberg (1927)</title>
                                        <citedRange unit="page" from="21" to="22"/>
                                    </bibl>, <bibl>
                                        <title ref="https://www.manuscripta.se/bibl/107624" type="short">Husmann (1964)</title>
                                        <citedRange unit="page" from="191" to="191"/>
                                    </bibl>.</p>
                            </origin>
                        </history>
